 * Linked List Theory
 *
 * =========================
 * 1. What is a Linked List?
 * =========================
 * A Linked List is a linear data structure where elements (nodes)
 * are not stored contiguously in memory.
 *
 * Each node contains:
 *  - data
 *  - reference (pointer) to the next node
 *
 * class Node {
 *     int data;
 *     Node next;
 * }
 *
 * =========================
 * 2. Array vs Linked List
 * =========================
 * Array:
 *  - Fixed size
 *  - Contiguous memory
 *  - Fast access O(1)
 *  - Insert/Delete costly
 *
 * Linked List:
 *  - Dynamic size
 *  - Non-contiguous memory
 *  - Access O(n)
 *  - Insert/Delete easy
 *
 * =========================
 * 3. Types of Linked Lists
 * =========================
 * 1) Singly Linked List
 *    data -> next
 *
 * 2) Doubly Linked List
 *    prev <- data -> next
 *
 * 3) Circular Linked List
 *    last node points to head
 *
 * =========================
 * 4. Basic Operations
 * =========================
 *
 * Traversal:
 * while (temp != null) {
 *     temp = temp.next;
 * }
 *
 * Insertion at beginning:
 * newNode.next = head;
 * head = newNode;
 *
 * Insertion at end:
 * tail.next = newNode;
 * tail = newNode;
 *
 * Deletion at beginning:
 * head = head.next;
 *
 * =========================
 * 5. Fast & Slow Pointer
 * =========================
 * Used for:
 *  - Find middle
 *  - Detect cycle
 *
 * while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
 *     slow = slow.next;
 *     fast = fast.next.next;
 * }
 *
 * =========================
 * 6. Important Problems
 * =========================
 * - Reverse Linked List
 * - Find Middle
 * - Detect Cycle (Floyd)
 * - Remove Nth Node from End
 * - Merge Two Sorted Lists
 * - Palindrome Linked List
 * - Odd Even Linked List
 * - Add Two Numbers (LC 2 & 445)
 *
 * =========================
 * 7. Dummy Node Technique
 * =========================
 * ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
 * ListNode temp = dummy;
 *
 * Helps avoid edge cases with head.
 *
 * =========================
 * 8. Time Complexity
 * =========================
 * Traversal: O(n)
 * Insert/Delete (known node): O(1)
 * Search: O(n)
 * Reverse: O(n)
 *
 * =========================
 * 9. Common Mistakes
 * =========================
 * - Forgetting null checks
 * - Using || instead of &&
 * - Losing head reference
 * - Creating cycles accidentally
 *
 * =========================
 * 10. Golden Rules
 * =========================
 * - Always draw pointers
 * - Change links before moving pointers
 * - Handle edge cases first
 *
